<div class="path-head"><p class="featured-region">Reading path · 10 stops</p><h1 class="path-title">Faiths of the Persian World</h1><p class="path-tagline">Zarathustra’s faith, the Persian empires that kept it, and the religions that grew around them.</p><p class="path-intro">Iran was the home of one of the world’s oldest revealed religions. Zarathustra taught a struggle between truth and the lie under the Wise Lord, Ahura Mazda, and his faith was the religion of the Achaemenid kings and the state religion of the Parthian and Sasanian empires. Under the Sasanians the prophet Mani founded a religion of light that spread from Roman North Africa to China. After the Arab conquest Zoroastrians became a minority, and some settled in Gujarat as the Parsis. This path follows these faiths and their neighbours, from the Roman cult of Mithras to the Yazidis of Lalish and the Bahá’í Faith, born in 19th-century Iran.</p><p class="path-types">Suits <a href="/start">the Thinker</a> · <a href="/start">the Chronicler</a></p></div>
<ol class="path-steps"><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/zoroaster"><span class="path-step-ph">1</span><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">1 · Encyclopedia · Person · c. 1200–1000 BCE (disputed)</span><span class="path-step-name">Zoroaster</span><span class="path-step-why">The prophet whose hymns, the Gathas, stand at the origin of Zoroastrianism; his dates are disputed by a thousand years, most scholars favouring the late second millennium BCE.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/religion/zoroastrianism"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/fire-temple.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">2 · Profile · c. 1200 BCE – present</span><span class="path-step-name">Zoroastrianism</span><span class="path-step-why">Faith of the Achaemenid kings and state religion of the Parthians and Sasanians, whose ideas of judgement, heaven and hell may have influenced Judaism, Christianity and Islam.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/ahura-mazda"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ahura-mazda.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">3 · Encyclopedia · Idea</span><span class="path-step-name">Ahura Mazda</span><span class="path-step-why">The Wise Lord, first notably invoked in the Behistun Inscription of Darius the Great and later named in royal inscriptions in a triad with Mithra and Anahita.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/mithra"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mithra.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">4 · Encyclopedia · Idea</span><span class="path-step-name">Mithra</span><span class="path-step-why">An Indo-Iranian god of covenant, truth and light, venerated in the Avesta and shown on Kushan coins, distinct from, though related to, the Mithras of Rome.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/religion/mithraism"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/mithra.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">5 · Profile · c. 80 CE – c. 400 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Mithraism</span><span class="path-step-why">A Roman mystery cult of soldiers and merchants who met in cave-like temples; Cumont saw it as the Roman form of Persian religion, a model scholars have generally rejected since the 1970s.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/empire/sasanian-empire"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/ardashir-i.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">6 · Profile · 224 CE – 651 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Sasanian Empire</span><span class="path-step-why">A centralised Zoroastrian monarchy that ruled Iran and Mesopotamia from 224 to 651 and was Rome’s great rival for four centuries, until the Arab conquests ended it.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/religion/manichaeism"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/bogu-qaghan.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">7 · Profile · c. 240 CE – c. 1600 CE</span><span class="path-step-name">Manichaeism</span><span class="path-step-why">Founded by the prophet Mani in Sasanian Mesopotamia about 240, it taught a struggle of light and darkness and spread from Roman North Africa to China.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/encyclopedia/parsis"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/parsis.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">8 · Encyclopedia · People</span><span class="path-step-name">Parsis</span><span class="path-step-why">Descendants of Zoroastrians who left Iran after the Arab conquest, this community of western India preserved the old state faith of the Sasanian Empire.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/religion/yazidism"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/yazidis.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">9 · Profile · c. 1100 CE – present</span><span class="path-step-name">Yazidism</span><span class="path-step-why">Given its lasting form by Sheikh Adi at Lalish, the faith of the Peacock Angel remembers 74 campaigns of destruction, among them the genocide at Sinjar in 2014.</span></span></a></li><li><a class="path-step" href="/religion/bahai-faith"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/haifa.jpg" alt="" loading="lazy" width="96" height="96"><span class="path-step-body"><span class="path-step-kind">10 · Profile · 1844 CE – present</span><span class="path-step-name">Bahá'í Faith</span><span class="path-step-why">Heralded by the Báb in Shiraz in 1844 and founded by Bahá’u’lláh, it teaches the oneness of God, of religion and of humanity, and has been persecuted in Iran from its beginnings.</span></span></a></li></ol>
